  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "Which of the following sets are finite?\n\n- $P({Z,t,n})$\n- $P(Z)$\n- $P({{2}^{n}\u2223n\\in Z})$",
  "worked_solution": "The power set of a finite set is finite, while the power set of an infinite set is infinite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Set I is finite since the underlying set is finite. Notice that \n\n$$\nP({Z,t,n})={\u2205,\\,{Z},\\,{t},\\,{n},\\,{Z,t},{Z,n},\\,{t,n},\\,{Z,t,n}}\n$$\n\nhas cardinality $8.$\n\n- Set II is infinite since the underlying set $Z$ is infinite.\n\n- Set III is infinite since the underlying set ${{2}^{n}\u2223n\\in Z}$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
